<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chhattisgarh Feudatory States</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">19th century–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Chhattisgarh States</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> Feudatory States were a group of fourteen small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahanadi">Mahanadi</a> region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, including Kanker, Kawardha, Sakti and Sarangarh. Formerly tributary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a>, they came under British paramountcy, attached first to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a> and from 1933 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-states-agency">Eastern States Agency</a>. They merged into India in 1948.</p></div>
